In programming, the "while" and "for" loops are both used for iteration, but they serve different purposes and have different syntax.

A "while" loop continues to execute as long as a specified condition is true. The syntax for a while loop is as follows:

```
while (condition) {
    // code to be executed
}
```

On the other hand, a "for" loop is used when you need to execute a block of code repeatedly for a fixed number of times. The syntax for a for loop is as follows:

```
for (initialization; condition; increment) {
    // code to be executed
}
```

The main difference between the two is that a "while" loop requires manual increment or decrement of the loop variable, whereas a "for" loop automatically increments or decrements the loop variable.

For example, if you want to print the numbers from 1 to 5, you can use a "for" loop as follows:

```
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

On the other hand, you can use a "while" loop as follows:

```
int i = 1;
while (i <= 5) {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
}
```

Here is a simple Java program that prints all permutations of an array:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1, 2, 3};
        permutations(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
    }

    public static void permutations(int[] arr, int l, int r) {
        if (l == r)
            System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));
        else {
            for (int i = l; i <= r; i++) {
                swap(arr, l, i);
                permutations(arr, l + 1, r);
                swap(arr, l, i);
            }
        }
    }

    public static void swap(int[] arr, int i, int j) {
        int temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = temp;
    }
}
```

You can run this program with an array of your choice, and it will print all its permutations.

